# File:  azure_gauge.dpi
# Authors: Tyler Lesthaeghe
# Created: 2026-09-29
# Description:
#   Measures the depth-camera mesh against the line profiler on the part
#   itself.
# Derived From:
#
#########################################################################
# Revision Log
#
# 2026-09-29:  Initial Commit
#
#########################################################################
# Development Notes
#
#
#########################################################################
# ==============================================================================
# THE KEYENCE MEASURES HOW WRONG THE AZURE MESH IS, ON THE PART, WITH NO TOUCH-OFF
# ==============================================================================
# The Azure Kinect's depth reads every surface about 12.45 mm TOO FAR. Eight
# independent measurements spanning two targets and 20 mm of surface height
# give -11.6 to -13.6 mm, mean -12.4, sd 0.7. A contact touch-off agrees with
# the Keyence twice, 0.7 mm apart on ground steel and 0.13 mm apart on paper,
# and the URDF tree rules out any shared arm error -- so it is the depth value,
# and a single scalar correction takes 12 mm to under 1 mm over the whole
# field.
#
# THAT CORRECTION IS A CALIBRATION, AND CALIBRATIONS DRIFT. A drifted one is
# silent. A Keyence raster planned over a mesh that reads a surface-ground
# plate 13.6 mm low across it and 22 mm low at one end flies the head at 58 mm
# against a 57 mm hard limit, brings 26-39% of the plate back as points
# scattered over 16 mm instead of a flat, and leaves the head nearly in contact
# with the part. Every stage downstream reports success.
#
# keyence_standoff.dpi catches that DURING a raster: its pre-flight gate asks
# the sensor at the first pose and refuses a sweep whose mesh is more than
# KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT_M out. Nothing else measures the residual
# disagreement BEFORE a raster is planned, on the actual part, at several
# places, so that the number can be trusted, reported, or fed back. That is
# this file, and it is what makes the pipeline self-correcting: the Azure's
# constant correction is a number the work cell re-measures every session, in
# about a minute, with no human touch-off.
#
# THIS NEVER TOUCHES THE PART. It is an optical gauge. The Keyence is a
# triangulation profiler standing off 57..103 mm; nothing here contacts
# anything, nothing here requires a contact touch-off, and nothing here may
# ever grow one -- that is the standing rule keyence_standoff.dpi records and
# it applies with full force to a gauge whose whole point is that it runs
# unattended.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ARITHMETIC, RE-DERIVED FROM THE DATASHEET AND THE CONSTANTS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The LJ-V7080 measures a surface from CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[0] to
# CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[1] -- 57 to 103 mm from the head -- which is the
# CAL_KEYENCE_REF_DIST reference distance of 80 mm plus or minus 23 mm. The
# window is therefore 46 mm wide. The optical frame sits ON the reference
# plane, so _cal_pose_for_sensor(aim, q, distance, "lj_v7080_optical_frame")
# puts the head `CAL_KEYENCE_REF_DIST + distance` from `aim`.
#
# Place the head so that the MESH-PREDICTED surface sits at
#
#     start_standoff = 100 mm            (the reference distance + 20 mm)
#
# and define the one number this file exists to produce:
#
#     delta = z_keyence_measured - z_mesh        positive: THE MESH READS LOW
#
# The head was placed off the MESH, so the distance to the REAL surface is
#
#     actual standoff = start_standoff - delta
#
# and the grab returns something exactly while that lies inside the window:
#
#     57 <= 100 - delta <= 103        <=>        -3 mm <= delta <= +43 mm
#
# So ONE position at 100 mm tolerates delta anywhere from -3 to +43 mm. The
# cell's own bias is about +12.45 mm, a third of the way into that, which is
# why THE FIRST GRAB NORMALLY JUST WORKS and hands back delta directly.
#
# AND THE HEADROOM IS ON THE DANGEROUS SIDE ON PURPOSE. The direction that
# hurts is large POSITIVE delta -- the mesh reads low, so the head is placed
# too close -- and from 100 mm it takes 43 mm of mesh error to reach the 57 mm
# limit. At the 80 mm reference distance (what a raster commands) it takes 23,
# and the 22 mm drift described above spends all but one of those. Standing
# off at the far end of the window buys 20 mm of it back for nothing, because
# a surface beyond the FAR limit costs a retry and a surface inside the NEAR
# limit costs the part.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LADDER IS THE FALLBACK, NOT THE METHOD
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The only case the first position does not cover is a mesh that reads HIGH by
# more than 3 mm: the real surface is then beyond the far limit and the grab
# returns nothing at all. Step the head DOWN by AZURE_GAUGE_STEP_M and grab
# again. 20 mm is chosen because the window is 46 mm wide, so one step CANNOT
# SKIP OVER the surface -- consecutive windows overlap by 26 mm. In delta:
#
#     head at 100 mm    returns for    -3 .. +43 mm
#     head at  80 mm                  -23 .. +23 mm
#     head at  60 mm                  -43 ..  +3 mm
#                                     -------------
#     three rungs cover              -43 .. +43 mm, contiguous
#
# which is why AZURE_GAUGE_TRIES is 3. A fourth rung adds no coverage; it only
# walks the head further down.
#
# TWO RULES BOUND IT, AND THEY COME FROM THE TWO WAYS A NO-RETURN CAN HAPPEN.
# A grab that returns nothing proves delta is outside that rung's window, and
# does NOT say which side. The ladder acts on the far-side explanation, so:
#
#   RULE A -- never command a pose whose PREDICTED actual standoff is below
#     CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[0] + CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_MARGIN_M (62 mm). At the first
#     rung the prediction is the mesh at face value, so this refuses a caller
#     who asks to start at 60 mm. At later rungs the mesh has been REFUTED --
#     the previous rung proved the surface is beyond its far limit, so the mesh
#     reads high by at least (previous rung - 103 mm) -- and the prediction is
#     updated by that evidence, which puts every rung of the default ladder at
#     83 mm of predicted actual standoff rather than at its commanded value.
#
#   RULE B -- when the sensor DID return something and its own heights say the
#     surface is inside the NEAR limit, stop. The near-side explanation is then
#     not a hypothesis, it is a measurement, and stepping down is exactly the
#     wrong move. check_profile() already computes this (its RANGE problem
#     carries standoff, standoff_min and standoff_max) and _cal_profile_points
#     leaves it in _CAL["last_profile_check"], so it costs nothing.
#
#   RULE C -- the contact floor, for the case where the sensor returns NOTHING
#     and near cannot be told from far. The near-side reading of the first
#     rung's silence is that the mesh reads LOW by at least
#     (start_standoff - 57 mm) = 43 mm; under THAT reading the head at rung k
#     is (s_k - 43) mm from the surface. Stop before that reaches zero. With
#     the defaults it permits 100, 80 and 60 and refuses 40, which is the same
#     three rungs the coverage argument gives and the same place
#     _cal_pose_for_sensor's own window check stops (a 40 mm head standoff is
#     outside 57..103 and it refuses to compute the pose at all).
#
# Three independent arguments, one answer: the ladder is 100, 80, 60 and then
# it says so and stops.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THREE POINTS AND NOT ONE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A drifted mesh is not uniformly wrong: 13.6 mm on average across a plate and
# about 22 mm at one end of the same plate. A single point measures the offset
# and calls the tilt zero; three non-collinear points measure both, and the
# residual about their plane says whether "one number plus a tilt" describes
# the disagreement at all. The points are taken from the mesh's OWN vertices,
# as a centre plus a ring, so each sample is guaranteed to lie on the surface.
#
# AND THEY ARE SPREAD OVER THE DETECTED REGION, NOT OVER THE MESH. A TSDF mesh
# extends as far as the sweep integrated, which is well past the part, so "the
# mesh's own extent" is the wrong extent: a ring spread over it lands 60-96 mm
# OUTSIDE the detected region and every sample measures the BENCH. The gauge
# then reports agreement to -0.00 mm about the TABLE and passes a mesh that is
# wrong everywhere the raster is about to fly, while the bench-to-part STEP
# leaks into the plane fit as a tilt of 18 mrad at sd 2.2 mm that is a tilt of
# nothing.
#
# The region that says where the part is exists at stage 2a -- stage 1 produced
# it and left it in `detected_region` -- so the gauge takes it, drops every
# vertex outside it before it chooses anything, and says which region it used.
# With no region anywhere it falls back to the mesh's extent, which is what a
# hand call with nothing but a PLY has to do.
#
# AND THE PLANE IS FITTED ACROSS THE SAMPLES, NEVER WITHIN A GRAB. One static
# profiler pose measures a LINE: 31 mm long, microns wide, every point at
# y == 0 in the sensor frame. fit_plane() handed a line returns the plane of
# least scatter, whose normal is the sensor's y axis, at a residual of exactly
# zero -- which is reported as "0.000 mm rms flat" at a tilt of 90.06 degrees,
# and means nothing. So each grab contributes ONE HEIGHT (the median world z of
# its points, which needs no plane and no support check), and the tilt comes
# from those heights against their xy. The lever is then the width of the part,
# not the width of a patch: verify_calibration's +/-45 mm patch cannot resolve
# its own 10 mrad criterion, and a 200 mm spread can.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PIPELINE IS NEVER ASKED TO CONFIRM A MOVE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO MOVE MADE BY THE AUTONOMOUS PIPELINE PROMPTS ANYONE. Prompting before a
# move belongs in the calibration routines, which have somebody at the
# keyboard; it does not belong in a pipeline run.
#
# _cal_fly_or_refuse(), which is verify_calibration()'s Keyence leg, goes
# capture_move() -> _decide() -> confirm() in cell_capture_node.dpi -- and that
# confirm() DELIBERATELY REFUSES TO BE AUTOMATED ON HARDWARE:
#
#     if CAPTURE_AUTO_CONFIRM and CELL_MODE != "sim":
#         raise CaptureRefused("... every motion on the production system is confirmed by
#             the operator.")
#
# That contract is RIGHT and nothing here weakens it. CAPTURE_AUTO_CONFIRM is
# not set, confirm() is not patched, and cell_capture_node.dpi is untouched. A
# capture scenario and a calibration run have somebody at the keyboard; they
# keep every prompt.
#
# The rule is about the ROUTE, not about the gate on it. The pipeline's own
# moves do not go through capture_move() at all: the Azure sweep and the
# Keyence raster fly `ros` directly -- ApproachInConfiguration,
# cartesian_move(), MoveJoints -- where `ros` is the session-wide cable guard
# install_cable_guard() put there. That path does not prompt. So the gauge has
# two routes, and the DEFAULT IS THE SILENT ONE:
#
#   confirm_moves=False (default, and what run_automated_scan gets)
#       _gauge_fly_unattended(): the guards asked explicitly, then the same
#       primitives the sweep uses. No prompt, no operator, nothing to answer.
#
#   confirm_moves=True (a hand call at the dgpy prompt, which IS calibration-ish)
#       _cal_fly_or_refuse(): previewed, confirmed, monitored.
#
# WHICH GUARDS THE SILENT ROUTE KEEPS, AND HOW. Removing a prompt must not
# remove a check, so each one is named and asked for explicitly BEFORE anything
# is commanded -- a refusal costs the sample point and the arm has not moved:
#
#   the work volume     _check_envelope() on the densified straight line, the
#                       same helper and the same 5 mm spacing capture_move()
#                       uses. This is the one guard the pipeline's `ros` does
#                       NOT carry -- install_cable_guard() asks one question and
#                       this is not it -- so the gauge asks it itself, and for
#                       the joint route it asks it again on the FK of every
#                       sampled configuration, which is what capture_move_joints
#                       does and is the only way a joint path's bulge is seen.
#   the cable envelope  _check_wrist3() on the configurations at both ends, at
#                       the tilt each one commands -- and then the guarded `ros`
#                       checks it a second time on the motion itself, because
#                       install_cable_guard() is in front of every call.
#   the speed limits    _speed_class() gives near/away and its limit in m/s;
#                       path_motion_scaling() converts that to the MoveIt
#                       scaling for THIS path, falling back to the work-cell-wide
#                       velocity curve -- the same two-step the sweep uses, and
#                       the reason it exists is that handing a speed through as
#                       a scaling flies it about 1.6x too fast.
#   the Keyence window  unchanged: keyence_standoff_gate() per rung plus RULES
#                       A, B and C above. Nothing about the prompt touched it.
#   the operator's stop scan_checkpoint() between points, which is how a sweep
#                       notices that External Control was stopped on the pendant.
#
# AND ONE IT DOES NOT. capture_move() wraps the motion in _Monitor, which
# watches TF in flight and stops the arm on a real overspeed. _Monitor is
# internal to cell_capture_motion.dpi and is not reachable from here, and the
# pipeline's own sweeps do not have it either -- every Azure and Keyence row
# goes out through the guarded `ros` with the scaling as its only speed bound.
# So the silent route keeps the speed LIMIT and not the in-flight watchdog,
# which is a statement about the pipeline and not about the gauge.
# confirm_moves=True is the route that has the watchdog.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT ELSE IT REUSES, AND WHY NOTHING HERE IS A NEW MOTION PRIMITIVE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every pose is placed by _cal_pose_for_sensor(). Every grab goes out through
# _cal_profile_points(), which drops the
# +Inf out-of-range columns (REP-117; pc2's skip_nans tests isnan and does not
# remove an infinity) and runs check_profile() -- so "the grab is not a
# measurement" arrives here as one CalibrationRefused with the reason in it,
# and is exactly the signal the ladder needs. The plan-time standoff gate is
# keyence_standoff_gate(), asked per rung with the near floor RULE A computes
# and the far end left open, because a gauge that deliberately stands off at
# the far end of the window is not a raster and must not be graded as one.
#
# Numpy and math only. No scipy, no open3d, no ROS except through the
# calibration helpers above. Python 3.10 and numpy 1.23 at the work cell.

import os
import json
import math
import time

import numpy as np

# A .dpi is compiled and executed as a FUNCTION BODY, and every .dpi shares one
# globals() dict -- so a top-level def or assignment binds a LOCAL of the
# include unless it is declared global first, invisible to every other module.
# dpi_exports_test.py refuses the pattern; everything defined below is listed
# here.
global AZURE_GAUGE_START_STANDOFF
global AZURE_GAUGE_POINTS
global AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM
global AZURE_GAUGE_STEP_M
global AZURE_GAUGE_TRIES
global AZURE_GAUGE_RING_FRACTION
global AZURE_GAUGE_EDGE_INSET_M
global AZURE_GAUGE_MIN_SPREAD_M
global AZURE_GAUGE_TF_TOL_M
global AZURE_GAUGE_ARRIVE_TOL_M
global AZURE_GAUGE_ACC_SCALING
global gauge_mesh_against_keyence
global gauge_standoff_rungs
global gauge_sample_points
global describe_gauge
global last_gauge_result
global _gauge_say
global _gauge_surface
global _gauge_region
global _gauge_plane_fit
global _gauge_profile_facts
global _gauge_finalise
global _gauge_save
global _gauge_move
global _gauge_stops
global _gauge_fly_unattended
global _gauge_scaling
global _gauge_accel
global _gauge_flew
global _gauge_joint_path_ok
global _GaugePath


#: Where the head is put for the FIRST grab, metres from the mesh-predicted
#: surface. The reference distance + 20 mm, i.e. the far end of the measuring
#: window, which is what buys the 43 mm of tolerance on the dangerous side.
#: See the header: -3 .. +43 mm of mesh error returns from here.
AZURE_GAUGE_START_STANDOFF = 0.100

#: How many places on the mesh to measure, by default. Three is the minimum
#: that gives an offset AND a tilt, and a drifted mesh has both.
AZURE_GAUGE_POINTS = 3

#: The disagreement a caller may gate on, mm. The same 2.0 mm
#: verify_calibration uses (CAL_VERIFY_DZ_MM), and for the same reason: a
#: single scalar correction takes this cell's 12.4 mm to under 1 mm, so 2 mm
#: passes a corrected cell with room and fails a drifted one.
AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM = 2.0

#: How far the head steps DOWN after a grab that returned nothing, metres.
#: Strictly less than the 46 mm window, so consecutive rungs overlap and the
#: surface cannot be stepped over.
AZURE_GAUGE_STEP_M = 0.020

#: How many rungs of that ladder. Three cover -43 .. +43 mm of mesh error and
#: a fourth adds no coverage. See RULE C in the header for what stops it.
AZURE_GAUGE_TRIES = 3

#: The sample ring's radius, as a fraction of the mesh's half-extent. 0.7
#: keeps the ring off the edge -- where a reconstructed mesh is worst and a
#: Noether raster runs off it anyway -- while spending most of the lever.
AZURE_GAUGE_RING_FRACTION = 0.7

#: And an absolute inset from the mesh's bounding box, metres, in case the
#: fraction above is generous on a small part.
AZURE_GAUGE_EDGE_INSET_M = 0.010

#: Below this xy spread the tilt is not reported at all, metres.
#:
#: NOT A STYLE CHOICE. verify_calibration measured 15.04 mrad against a
#: 10.0 mrad tolerance on a grab whose true full-field tilt was 1.06 mrad,
#: because 1522 points at 0.64 mm rms over a +/-45 mm patch cannot resolve
#: 10 mrad. A tilt is a height difference divided by a baseline, so a short
#: baseline turns sensor noise into an alarm. 40 mm of baseline with the
#: Keyence's 0.1 mm noise is about 2.5 mrad of resolution, which is the floor
#: at which the number starts meaning something.
AZURE_GAUGE_MIN_SPREAD_M = 0.040

#: How far the two independent readings of delta may disagree, metres.
#:
#: The world-frame reading subtracts the mesh's z from the transformed points'
#: z; the sensor-frame reading subtracts the measured standoff from the
#: commanded one. They are the same quantity from the two ends of the pipeline
#: and agree to the sensor's own noise unless TF and the commanded pose
#: disagree -- a stale transform, a description that moved since the pose was
#: computed, an arm that did not arrive. Reported, not refused: it is a note
#: about the work cell, and the world-frame number is the one used.
AZURE_GAUGE_TF_TOL_M = 0.001

#: How close the tool must land to the pose that was commanded before the
#: unattended route calls the move flown, metres.
#:
#: A LAST-RESORT TEST, NOT A PRECISION ONE. `result.success` is the authority
#: and is what the sweep reads; this is for the one call that may not carry a
#: success flag at all -- ros.MoveJoints falls back to a direct trajectory
#: command when there is no execute_joint_move server, and hands back that
#: goal's result instead. A gauge whose whole arithmetic assumes the head
#: arrived cannot read "unknown" as yes. 5 mm is far below the 20 mm
#: ladder step, so it cannot confuse one rung with another, and far above any
#: planner residual. The 1 mm agreement between the two readings of delta above
#: is the precision test, and it stays a note rather than a refusal.
AZURE_GAUGE_ARRIVE_TOL_M = 0.005

#: MoveIt ACCELERATION scaling floor for a gauge approach. NOT the velocity
#: scaling, which is what this used to be; see _gauge_accel() for the ramp-time
#: arithmetic that makes acc == vel wrong at any speed, and for the three
#: things that bound the replacement. 0.05 is RASTER_ACCEL, what the
#: eddy-current raster already flies while the probe is in contact with the
#: specimen, and half the 0.1 the reconstruction sweep flies in free space.
AZURE_GAUGE_ACC_SCALING = 0.05

#: The last result, for an operator who did not keep the return value.
last_gauge_result = None


def _gauge_say(say):
    """The reporting sink: the caller's, else the calibration module's."""
    if say is not None:
        return say
    fn = globals().get("_cal_say")
    return fn if fn is not None else (lambda msg: print(msg))


class _GaugePath(object):
    """The three attributes keyence_standoff_gate() reads off a toolpath.

    A DELIBERATE ADAPTER, NOT A SECOND PATH TYPE. The gate casts the sensor's
    own optical axis from every waypoint of a plan and measures how far the
    first surface is, and that is exactly the check a gauge pose needs -- it
    catches a nearer piece of the part between the head and the point that was
    aimed at, which no amount of arithmetic about the sampled vertex can. The
    gate wants positions, quats and segments; a gauge rung is one segment of
    one waypoint, so this says so rather than inventing a parallel check.
    """

    def __init__(self, positions, quats):
        self.positions = np.asarray(positions, float).reshape(-1, 3)
        self.quats = np.asarray(quats, float).reshape(-1, 4)
        self.segments = [(0, self.positions.shape[0])]
        self.source = "azure gauge"


def _gauge_surface(mesh=None):
    """(geom, what) for the surface the Azure believes in. Refuses a guess.

    `mesh` is anything _surface_geometry() accepts -- a PLY path, a
    (verts, tris) pair, read_ply_mesh()'s (verts, normals, tris), a float world
    z or {"plane_z": z} -- and defaults to the session's `rough_mesh`, which is
    what reconstruct_rough() leaves behind and what the Keyence raster is
    planned over. THE POINT OF THIS GAUGE IS THE MESH, so there is no sensible
    default surface and no fallback: with none, it refuses.
    """
    if mesh is None:
        mesh = globals().get("rough_mesh")
    if mesh is None:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "gauge_mesh_against_keyence() measures the disagreement between "
            "the AZURE MESH and the Keyence, and there is no mesh. Run "
            "reconstruct_rough() first, or pass mesh= a PLY on disk, a "
            "(verts, tris) pair, or a world z for a flat. Nothing has been "
            "guessed and the arm has not moved.")
    geom = _surface_geometry(mesh)
    if geom is None:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "the surface handed to gauge_mesh_against_keyence() came back as "
            "nothing. Pass mesh= a PLY on disk, a (verts, tris) pair, or a "
            "world z for a flat.")
    return geom, _describe_surface(mesh, geom)


def _gauge_region(region):
    """((xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax), where it came from), or (None, why not).

    THE REGION IS WHERE THE PART IS, AND THE MESH IS NOT. See the header:
    samples spread over a TSDF mesh, which extends as far as the sweep
    integrated, can all land on the bench outside the part.

    `region=None` takes `detected_region`, which stage 1 of run_automated_scan()
    sets on both its branches -- the detector's box, or the one a caller passed
    by hand -- and which is therefore exactly right at stage 2a with no change
    anywhere else. That is the same fallback keyence_standoff.dpi and
    mesh_reconstruction.dpi already use, for the same reason and spelled the
    same way. `region=False` says "the whole mesh" out loud, for a hand call on
    a PLY that has nothing to do with the session's last part.

    Validated through check_region() when that is loaded, because the one thing
    a region gets wrong is its ORDER: (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax) is what a person
    types, and the two orders differ silently. A gauge is not the place to
    invent a second opinion about that.
    """
    if region is False:
        return None, "the whole mesh (region=False)"
    where = "given to the gauge"
    if region is None:
        region = globals().get("detected_region")
        where = "the detected region"
    if region is None:
        return None, "the mesh's own extent (nothing has detected a region)"
    fn = globals().get("check_region")
    if fn is not None:
        region = fn(region, "the azure gauge")
        if region is None:
            return None, "the mesh's own extent"
    r = tuple(float(v) for v in region)
    if len(r) != 4:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "region= for the azure gauge must be (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) in "
            "world metres, not %d values." % (len(r),))
    return r, where


def _gauge_scaling(poses, v_limit):
    """(MoveIt velocity scaling for `poses` at `v_limit` m/s, where it came from).

    THE SAME TWO-STEP THE SWEEP USES. CartesianMove's `vel` is a fraction of the
    joint velocity limits and not a speed, and on this cell the mapping between
    them is a curve, not a constant: handing a speed straight through as a
    scaling flew a 28 mm/s sweep at ~40. This path's own kinematics decide it
    when IK along the path can be had (path_motion_scaling(), which is derived
    FROM the limit), and the cell-wide velocity curve decides it when they
    cannot -- which is exactly the order _reconstruct() and capture_move() both
    settled on, and for the measurements behind it see path_motion_scaling().
    """
    per_path = globals().get("path_motion_scaling")
    if per_path is not None:
        try:
            s = per_path(poses, float(v_limit))
        except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
            s = None
        if s is not None:
            return float(s), "this path's own IK"
    curve = globals().get("motion_scaling")
    if curve is not None:
        try:
            return float(curve(float(v_limit))), "the cell-wide velocity curve"
        except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
            pass
    return float(v_limit), "the limit itself (this cell has no velocity curve)"


def _gauge_accel(vel):
    """MoveIt ACCELERATION scaling for a gauge approach, given its velocity scaling.

    NOT `vel`. Passing the velocity scaling through as the acceleration scaling
    is what this used to do, and it is a different mistake from passing a speed
    through as a scaling -- it is not wrong about the units, it is wrong about
    which limit the number belongs to. Velocity scaling and acceleration scaling
    are independent fractions of two different joint limits (120-180 deg/s and
    5.0 rad/s^2 on this arm, tari_ur_description/config/joint_limits.yaml), and
    tying them together makes the RAMP TIME a constant:

        t_ramp = (vel * v_max) / (acc * a_max) = v_max / a_max  when acc == vel

    which on this arm is 0.42-0.63 s no matter how slow the move is. At the
    gauge's own scaling -- about 0.0032 for the 10 mm/s near-the-part class --
    a 56-60 mm descent therefore spends over a second of its ~7 s ramping, and
    it is the only class in the pipeline whose worst joint velocity reaches only
    11-16% of its scaled limit: it never settles at cruise. The three gauge
    points in results16 took 192.1 s, 36.5 s and 65.5 s, with the SHORTER of
    two moves (56.6 mm against 60.0 mm) taking 1.8x longer -- the signature of
    a profile that is all ramp, where duration goes as sqrt(length) rather than
    as length. (Most of that 192 s was the planner's unconditional time-stretch,
    which divided accelerations by k^2; that is fixed in tari_cpp_mtc and only
    the fallback path pays it now. The ramp is what is left, and it is this
    number's.)

    WHAT BOUNDS IT, since this is a probe approaching a part and 0.1 is not a
    number to copy from somewhere else:

    1. It cannot raise the peak speed, so it is not what the near-the-part speed
       rule constrains. The velocity scaling bounds joint speed directly and
       tari_cpp_mtc's checkTrajectory() enforces both that and max_tip_speed in
       metres per second at the tool. Whatever this is, the descent still runs
       at the speed class _speed_class() computed for it.
    2. Higher is SAFER on the only measure that matters close to a surface.
       Travel after a stop is commanded is v^2/(2a): at the 10 mm/s class that
       is 3.1 mm at acceleration scaling 0.0032 and 0.2 mm at 0.05. Tying
       acceleration to a small velocity does not make the approach gentle, it
       makes it unable to stop.
    3. The ceiling is evidence rather than arithmetic. 0.05 is RASTER_ACCEL,
       what the eddy-current raster flies while the probe is IN CONTACT with the
       specimen, and it is half the 0.1 the reconstruction sweep flies in free
       space. A non-contact descent to a 100 mm standoff is strictly less
       hazardous than a contact pass, so 0.05 is a value already flown near this
       part rather than one borrowed from free space. 0.1 would shorten the ramp
       by a further 0.02 s and has evidence only in free space, so it is not
       taken.

    max() rather than a plain constant: a fast away-from-the-part leg may be
    authorised a velocity scaling above this, and giving such a move LESS
    acceleration than velocity would put the long ramp straight back.
    """
    return max(float(vel), float(AZURE_GAUGE_ACC_SCALING))


def _gauge_flew(res, xyz, label):
    """(did that move get the head there, why not). Never raises.

    THE RESULT FIRST, ARRIVAL AS THE BACKSTOP. `result.success` is what
    _fly_scanpath() reads off every sweep row and it is the authority whenever
    it is there. ros.MoveJoints does not always carry one -- with no
    execute_joint_move server it falls back to a direct trajectory command and
    returns that goal's result -- and a gauge whose arithmetic assumes the head
    arrived must not read "no success flag" as success. So when there is none,
    live TF is asked instead, against AZURE_GAUGE_ARRIVE_TOL_M.
    """
    r = getattr(res, "result", None)
    if r is not None and hasattr(r, "success"):
        if bool(getattr(r, "success", False)):
            return True, ""
        frac = float(getattr(r, "fraction", 0.0) or 0.0)
        if frac >= 0.999:
            return False, ("planned in full, EXECUTION refused -- mtc_node's "
                           "log says why")
        if frac <= 1e-9:
            return False, ("the planner reached none of it, or a guard refused "
                           "it (the refusal is printed above)")
        return False, "the planner reached %.0f%% of the move" % (100.0*frac,)
    if res is None:
        return False, "the motion module refused the target"
    snap = live_pose.snapshot()
    if snap is None:
        return False, ("the move returned no result and there is no TF for "
                       "probe_end, so whether it arrived is unknown")
    d = float(np.linalg.norm(np.asarray(snap[0], float).reshape(3)
                             - np.asarray(xyz, float).reshape(3)))
    if d <= float(AZURE_GAUGE_ARRIVE_TOL_M):
        return True, ""
    return False, ("the move returned no success flag and the tool is %.1f mm "
                   "from where it was sent, over the %.1f mm this counts as "
                   "arrived" % (1e3*d, 1e3*float(AZURE_GAUGE_ARRIVE_TOL_M)))


def _gauge_joint_path_ok(q0, q1, label):
    """Check a joint move end to end BEFORE it is commanded. Raises, or returns.

    THE WHOLE POINT IS THE BULGE. A joint move's tip does not travel in a
    straight line, so checking its endpoints against the work volume checks
    nothing about where the tooling actually goes -- and this route exists
    precisely for the poses whose straight line would not plan. So it is the
    check capture_move_joints() makes, made the same way: sample the
    interpolated joint path every 2 degrees of the largest joint change, run FK
    on every sample, and hold the tip to the envelope and wrist 3 to the band
    for the tilt FK says each configuration commands.

    NO FK MEANS NO MOVE. capture_move_joints() refuses a move it cannot check
    ("the move cannot be checked, so it is not made") and so does this: an
    unchecked joint path near a part is the thing the guards are for.
    """
    a = np.asarray(q0, float).reshape(6)
    b = np.asarray(q1, float).reshape(6)
    dq = np.abs(b - a)
    n = int(min(60, max(8, math.degrees(float(dq.max()))/2.0)))
    path = [a + t*(b - a) for t in np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, n)]
    node = globals().get("capture_node")
    fks = None
    if node is not None:
        try:
            fks = [node.fk(q, links=("probe_end",)) for q in path]
        except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
            fks = None
    if fks is None or any(f is None for f in fks):
        raise CaptureRefused(
            "%s: /compute_fk did not answer, so the joint path cannot be "
            "checked against the work volume and it is not made" % (label,))
    tip = np.array([f[0][0] for f in fks], float)
    _check_envelope(tip, label)
    _check_wrist3([list(q) for q in path], label, quats=[f[0][1] for f in fks])
    return tip


def _gauge_fly_unattended(xyz, quat, label):
    """Get the head to one gauge pose ON THE PIPELINE'S MOTION PATH. NO PROMPT.

    THIS IS THE DEFAULT AND IT IS THE DEFAULT ON PURPOSE. It is what
    run_automated_scan() gets, and the autonomous pipeline never asks an
    operator to approve a move. Nothing here reaches
    confirm(), sets CAPTURE_AUTO_CONFIRM or touches cell_capture_node.dpi: the
    primitives below are the ones the Azure sweep and the Keyence raster already
    fly, and they have never prompted.

    Returns _cal_fly_to()'s dict shape -- {"ok", "route", "why", "tried"} -- so
    it is interchangeable with _cal_fly_or_refuse() at the call site, and raises
    CalibrationRefused when no route reached the pose, which costs THAT SAMPLE
    POINT and not the run.

    EVERY GUARD IS ASKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS COMMANDED. The work volume, the
    cable band and the speed limit are checked first, so a refusal happens with
    the arm still where it was; see the header for which guard is which and for
    the one thing this route does not have (_Monitor's in-flight watchdog, which
    the pipeline's own sweeps do not have either).
    """
    # cell_capture_motion.dpi's guards, by name, because losing one silently is
    # the failure this whole file exists to catch in another form. With any of
    # them missing there is nothing to check the work volume WITH, so this
    # refuses rather than flying unchecked -- and names the confirmed route,
    # which needs the same module anyway but at least says so to a human.
    need = ("_check_envelope", "_check_wrist3", "_speed_class", "_cap_densify",
            "_cap_pose", "_ik_target", "capture_node", "live_pose",
            "CaptureRefused", "ros")
    missing = [k for k in need if globals().get(k) is None]
    if missing:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "%s: the unattended gauge route needs %s, and this session has "
            "none of them -- so nothing would check the work volume. Include "
            "cell_capture.dpi, or call "
            "gauge_mesh_against_keyence(confirm_moves=True) to fly it the "
            "confirmed way with an operator at the keyboard. The arm has not "
            "moved." % (label, ", ".join(missing)))
    x1 = np.asarray(xyz, float).reshape(3)
    q1 = tuple(float(v) for v in quat)
    snap = live_pose.snapshot()
    if snap is None:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "%s: there is no TF for probe_end, so where the arm is now is "
            "unknown and the path to this pose cannot be checked against the "
            "work volume. Is the robot driver publishing joint_states? Nothing "
            "moved." % (label,))
    x0 = np.asarray(snap[0], float).reshape(3)
    q0 = tuple(float(v) for v in snap[1])
    tried = []

    # -- THE GUARDS ---------------------------------------------------------
    # WHAT IS CHECKED HERE IS WHAT IS CERTAIN: the straight line's envelope, the
    # speed class, and the arm's CURRENT wrist angle, which is a fact and not a
    # prediction.
    #
    # The destination's wrist angle is a prediction, and it is checked as one --
    # `local_only=True`, so the branch judged is the branch the node that will
    # fly the move would choose. A PREDICTION MADE IN A MODE THE EXECUTOR IS NOT
    # IN IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THE EXECUTOR: whatever _ik_target() offers under
    # the `transit` profile is move_group in GLOBAL mode, free to ignore the
    # seed, and it will happily return the out-of-band branch (-178.5 deg where
    # the in-band equivalent is +181.5) for a small lateral step, which refuses
    # the move on both routes before either is tried.
    #
    # AND A PREDICTION THAT DID NOT COME BACK REFUSES NOTHING. Local IK failing
    # to converge says nothing about the wrist; route 1 is guarded on the motion
    # itself by the cable-guarded `ros`, and route 2 checks its whole
    # interpolated path by FK before it commands anything.
    q_pred = None
    try:
        samples = _cap_densify([x0, x1])
        _check_envelope(samples, label)
        cls, vmax = _speed_class(samples)
        q_here = capture_node.current_joints()
        try:
            q_pred = _ik_target(x1, q1, local_only=True)
        except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
            q_pred = None
        pairs = []
        if q_here is not None:
            pairs.append(([float(v) for v in q_here], q0))
        if q_pred is not None:
            pairs.append(([float(v) for v in q_pred], q1))
        if pairs:
            _check_wrist3([p for p, _q in pairs], label,
                          quats=[q for _p, q in pairs])
    except CaptureRefused as e:
        raise CalibrationRefused("%s: %s" % (label, str(e).splitlines()[0]))

    # -- 1. THE STRAIGHT LINE, on the pipeline's own primitive ---------------
    # cartesian_move() rather than ros.CartesianMove, which is what every scan
    # call site uses: it picks the IK settings this KIND of move needs, and a
    # single-waypoint step wants the tight threshold (0.01 mm rather than 0.1,
    # or the step lands short). `ros` is the cable guard, so the band is checked
    # a second time on the motion itself.
    vel, how = _gauge_scaling([(x0, q0), (x1, q1)], vmax)
    move = globals().get("cartesian_move") or ros.CartesianMove
    res = move([_cap_pose(x1, q1)], vel=vel, acc=_gauge_accel(vel),
               min_fraction=1.0)
    ok, why = _gauge_flew(res, x1, label)
    if ok:
        return {"ok": True, "route": "cartesian", "why": None, "tried": tried,
                "scaling": vel, "scaling_from": how, "speed_class": cls}
    tried.append(("cartesian", why))

    # -- 2. THE SAME ENDPOINT, REACHED IN JOINT SPACE ------------------------
    # verify_calibration's own second route and for its own reason: a leg that
    # will not plan as a straight line usually plans in joint space, and only
    # the ENDPOINT of a gauge move is measured, so any route that arrives is as
    # good as any other. Checked first, by FK, over the whole path.
    # THE GLOBAL IK FALLBACK BELONGS HERE AND ONLY HERE. A branch the arm is not
    # in is a legitimate thing to TRY when the straight line would not plan --
    # only the endpoint of a gauge move is measured, so any route that arrives is
    # as good as another -- and _gauge_joint_path_ok() checks the whole
    # interpolated path by FK before anything is commanded. It is NOT a
    # legitimate thing to refuse the move on, which is why the guards above ask
    # local IK only.
    q_there = q_pred
    if q_there is None:
        try:
            q_there = _ik_target(x1, q1)
        except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
            q_there = None
    if q_there is None:
        tried.append(("joint move", "no IK solution for the pose from here"))
    else:
        q_here = capture_node.current_joints()
        if q_here is None:
            tried.append(("joint move", "joint_states are not arriving"))
        else:
            try:
                _gauge_joint_path_ok(q_here, q_there, label)
            except CaptureRefused as e:
                tried.append(("joint move", str(e).splitlines()[0]))
            else:
                jmk = (globals().get("joint_move_kwargs") or (lambda: {}))()
                # At least as long as travelling the straight line at the class
                # limit would take, so the duration asked for cannot itself be
                # the thing that exceeds the limit. JOINT_MOVE_SCALING is the
                # cell's own bound on top, which is what every automated joint
                # move in the pipeline goes at.
                secs = max(4.0, float(np.linalg.norm(x1 - x0))/max(vmax, 1e-3))
                # min_duration_s, not secs alone. `secs` is honoured only by the
                # direct fallback, and it is a DEFAULT on most paths -- making it
                # binding everywhere turned ApproachInConfiguration's secs=6.0
                # into a six-second crawl for every raster-row approach. This is
                # one of the few places a duration bound is actually computed
                # rather than defaulted, so this is where it is asked for.
                res = ros.MoveJoints([float(v) for v in q_there], secs=secs,
                                     min_duration_s=secs, plan=True, **jmk)
                ok, why = _gauge_flew(res, x1, label)
                if ok:
                    return {"ok": True, "route": "joint move", "why": None,
                            "tried": tried, "scaling": vel,
                            "scaling_from": how, "speed_class": cls}
                tried.append(("joint move", why))
    # ONE LINE, and every route on it. The caller records
    # str(e).splitlines()[0] against the sample point -- which is right, a
    # sample's `why` is one line -- so a refusal whose reasons are on the second
    # and third lines arrives as "it did not reach it" and nothing else.
    # _cal_fly_or_refuse()'s multi-line form is for an operator reading a
    # traceback; this one has to survive being stored.
    raise CalibrationRefused(
        "%s: the arm did not reach this pose by any route (%s), so nothing has "
        "been measured there."
        % (label, "; ".join("%s: %s" % t for t in tried) or "no route to try"))


def _gauge_stops():
    """The exception types that END THE RUN rather than costing one point.

    CaptureStopped (the arm was physically stopped and a human has to look at
    the cell), CaptureAbort (the operator pressed q), KeyboardInterrupt -- and
    ScanAborted, WHICH THE UNATTENDED ROUTE NEEDS. With no prompt to answer, the
    operator's only control is the pendant, and scan_checkpoint() is what turns
    that into a stop. It raises ScanAborted, which is none of the other three --
    so without it here the partial result would be neither finalised nor saved
    and `last_gauge_result` would still hold the previous run's.

    Resolved at call time because robot_supervisor.dpi is optional: a config
    without it has no ScanAborted to name, and the other three still apply.
    """
    out = [CaptureStopped, CaptureAbort, KeyboardInterrupt]
    t = globals().get("ScanAborted")
    if isinstance(t, type) and issubclass(t, BaseException):
        out.append(t)
    return tuple(out)


def _gauge_move(xyz, quat, label, confirm_moves=False):
    """Fly one gauge pose. UNATTENDED BY DEFAULT; confirmed when asked.

    The one place the two routes are chosen between, so there is exactly one
    answer to "did this run prompt?" and it is a parameter rather than a mode.
    See the header.
    """
    if confirm_moves:
        return _cal_fly_or_refuse(xyz, quat, label)
    return _gauge_fly_unattended(xyz, quat, label)


def gauge_sample_points(geom, n=None, ring_fraction=None, inset=None,
                        envelope=None, region=None):
    """`n` points on the mesh, spread over the PART. (n, 3) world.

    TAKEN FROM THE MESH'S OWN VERTICES, and that is the whole trick: a vertex
    is guaranteed to lie on the surface, so its z IS the mesh's prediction
    there and no ray cast, no interpolation and no search band is involved. The
    targets are a centre plus a ring at AZURE_GAUGE_RING_FRACTION of the
    half-extent; each target takes the nearest vertex in xy.

    `region` is (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) in world metres, or None for the mesh's
    whole extent. SPREADING OVER THE MESH'S EXTENT IS A TRAP: a TSDF mesh
    reaches as far as the sweep integrated, so the ring can land 60-96 mm
    outside the detected region, every point measures the BENCH, and the gauge
    reports -0.00 mm about a surface the raster is never going to fly over.
    Vertices outside the region are dropped BEFORE the extent is computed, so
    the centre, the radius and every chosen vertex are the part's -- not clipped
    afterwards, which would move the ring rather than resize it. None spreads
    over the whole mesh, because a hand call with nothing but a PLY has no
    region to be given.

    A ring rather than a line, because three collinear points measure a tilt in
    one direction and call the other zero. A centre as well, from n >= 4, so the
    residual about the fitted plane can see a bowl -- the Azure's own field has
    one, 0.64 mm centre to 0.36 mm edge over 645 x 848 mm.

    `envelope` is capture_envelope()'s dict, or None. Vertices outside the work
    volume in xy are dropped before anything is chosen: "the mesh's reachable
    extent" is the part of it the arm may be over, and the arm cannot reach a
    part of the mesh that sticks out of the volume. The refusal that matters is
    still capture_move()'s, on the pose, at flight time.
    """
    n = int(AZURE_GAUGE_POINTS if n is None else n)
    if n < 1:
        raise CalibrationRefused("gauge_mesh_against_keyence(n=%d): there is "
                                 "nothing to measure." % (n,))
    frac = float(AZURE_GAUGE_RING_FRACTION if ring_fraction is None
                 else ring_fraction)
    inset = float(AZURE_GAUGE_EDGE_INSET_M if inset is None else inset)
    if geom[0] != "mesh":
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "the surface is %s, which has no extent to spread sample points "
            "over -- a plane is infinite and carries no vertices. Pass "
            "points=[(x, y), ...] (or (x, y, z) triples) to say where to "
            "measure." % ("a horizontal plane at z %.4f" % (geom[1],)
                          if geom[0] == "plane" else geom[0],))
    V = np.asarray(geom[1], float).reshape(-1, 3)
    if V.shape[0] < n:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "the mesh has %d vertices and %d sample points were asked for."
            % (V.shape[0], n))
    keep = np.ones(V.shape[0], bool)
    if envelope is not None:
        lo, hi = envelope["lo"], envelope["hi"]
        keep = ((V[:, 0] >= lo[0]) & (V[:, 0] <= hi[0]) &
                (V[:, 1] >= lo[1]) & (V[:, 1] <= hi[1]))
        if int(keep.sum()) < n:
            raise CalibrationRefused(
                "only %d of the mesh's %d vertices are inside the work volume "
                "in xy, and %d sample points were asked for. The part is "
                "outside the measured volume, or the volume is wrong: "
                "capture_touch_off() measures it."
                % (int(keep.sum()), V.shape[0], n))
    if region is not None:
        r = [float(v) for v in region]
        inr = ((V[:, 0] >= r[0]) & (V[:, 0] <= r[2]) &
               (V[:, 1] >= r[1]) & (V[:, 1] <= r[3]))
        if int((keep & inr).sum()) < n:
            raise CalibrationRefused(
                "only %d of the mesh's %d vertices are inside the region "
                "x %.3f..%.3f y %.3f..%.3f, and %d sample points were asked "
                "for. The mesh and the region describe different parts -- a "
                "region left over from an earlier run, or a mesh of something "
                "else. Pass region= the right one, or region=False to spread "
                "the samples over the whole mesh (which on a TSDF mesh can put "
                "every one of them on the bench). Nothing has moved."
                % (int((keep & inr).sum()), V.shape[0],
                   r[0], r[2], r[1], r[3], n))
        keep = keep & inr
    P = V[keep]
    x0, x1 = float(P[:, 0].min()), float(P[:, 0].max())
    y0, y1 = float(P[:, 1].min()), float(P[:, 1].max())
    cx, cy = 0.5*(x0 + x1), 0.5*(y0 + y1)
    # The radius is the tighter of the two rules and is never negative.
    r = max(0.0, min(frac*0.5*(x1 - x0) - inset, frac*0.5*(y1 - y0) - inset))
    targets = []
    if n >= 4:
        targets.append((cx, cy))
    ring = n - len(targets)
    # Offset the first spoke off the axes so a rectangular part does not put
    # two of three points on the same row.
    for k in range(ring):
        a = 2.0*math.pi*k/ring + math.pi/6.0
        targets.append((cx + r*math.cos(a), cy + r*math.sin(a)))
    chosen, taken = [], set()
    for tx, ty in targets:
        d = (P[:, 0] - tx)**2 + (P[:, 1] - ty)**2
        order = np.argsort(d, kind="stable")
        pick = None
        for i in order[:max(64, n*8)]:
            if int(i) not in taken:
                pick = int(i)
                break
        if pick is None:
            pick = int(order[0])
        taken.add(pick)
        chosen.append(P[pick])
    return np.asarray(chosen, float).reshape(-1, 3)


def gauge_standoff_rungs(start=None, step=None, tries=None, range_m=None,
                         margin=None):
    """The step-down ladder, and the rules that bound it.

    Returns (rungs, stop_reason): the commanded head standoffs in metres, in
    flight order, and why there are not more of them. Raises
    CalibrationRefused when the FIRST rung is already unsafe -- that is a
    caller error, not a short ladder.

    Every number here is derived in the header; this is where the derivation is
    executable. Nothing is hardcoded: the window and the margin come from
    CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M and CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_MARGIN_M.
    """
    lo, hi = (float(CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[0]), float(CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[1])) \
        if range_m is None else (float(range_m[0]), float(range_m[1]))
    m = float(CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_MARGIN_M if margin is None else margin)
    s0 = float(AZURE_GAUGE_START_STANDOFF if start is None else start)
    dz = float(AZURE_GAUGE_STEP_M if step is None else step)
    k_max = int(AZURE_GAUGE_TRIES if tries is None else tries)
    width = hi - lo
    if dz <= 0.0:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "a step of %.1f mm is not a step down. The ladder only ever moves "
            "the head CLOSER, and only after the sensor has proved the surface "
            "is beyond its far limit." % (1e3*dz,))
    if dz >= width:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "a step of %.1f mm over a %.0f mm measuring window can SKIP THE "
            "SURFACE: consecutive rungs would not overlap, so a mesh error "
            "between two of them returns nothing at either and the gauge "
            "concludes the wrong thing. Use less than %.0f mm."
            % (1e3*dz, 1e3*width, 1e3*width))
    rungs, why = [], ""
    s = s0
    for k in range(max(0, k_max)):
        # RULE A. What the actual standoff is predicted to be, given every
        # no-return so far. At the first rung that is the mesh at face value;
        # afterwards the mesh has been refuted on the far side and the bound on
        # how LOW it can read is (previous rung - hi), which is negative.
        delta_max = 0.0 if not rungs else (rungs[-1] - hi)
        predicted = s - delta_max
        if predicted < lo + m:
            if not rungs:
                raise CalibrationRefused(
                    "a start standoff of %.1f mm would put the head %.1f mm "
                    "from the surface the mesh predicts, inside the %.0f mm "
                    "near limit plus its %.0f mm margin. The LJ-V7080 returns "
                    "WRONG VALUES at the bottom of its range rather than "
                    "failing, and that is what this gauge exists to catch: it "
                    "has cost 26-39%% of a plate. Start at %.0f mm (the "
                    "reference distance + 20 mm) and let the ladder come down."
                    % (1e3*s, 1e3*predicted, 1e3*lo, 1e3*m,
                       1e3*AZURE_GAUGE_START_STANDOFF))
            why = ("the next rung at %.0f mm would predict an actual standoff "
                   "of %.0f mm, inside the %.0f mm near limit plus its %.0f mm "
                   "margin" % (1e3*s, 1e3*predicted, 1e3*lo, 1e3*m))
            break
        # RULE C. The contact floor. The other reading of the FIRST rung's
        # silence is a mesh that reads low by at least (s0 - lo); under it the
        # head at this rung is (s - (s0 - lo)) from the surface. Stop before
        # that reaches the margin.
        if rungs:
            worst = s - (rungs[0] - lo)
            if worst < m:
                why = ("a rung at %.0f mm is refused: if the no-returns so far "
                       "were the NEAR side rather than the far side -- which a "
                       "profiler that returns nothing cannot distinguish -- the "
                       "mesh reads at least %.0f mm low and the head would be "
                       "%.1f mm from the surface"
                       % (1e3*s, 1e3*(rungs[0] - lo), 1e3*worst))
                break
        rungs.append(s)
        s -= dz
    else:
        if k_max > 0:
            covered_lo = rungs[-1] - hi
            why = ("%d rung(s) are available; between them they return for a "
                   "mesh error of %+.0f .. %+.0f mm"
                   % (len(rungs), 1e3*covered_lo, 1e3*(rungs[0] - lo)))
    if not rungs:
        raise CalibrationRefused(
            "tries=%d leaves no pose to fly." % (k_max,))
    return rungs, why


def _gauge_profile_facts(pts_s, ref, window):
    """What one grab says about itself, in the sensor's own frame.

    `standoff = ref_dist - z` is the convention keyence_driver_node packs and
    keyence_profile_standoff() documents: positive z is TOWARDS the head, so
    z = +0.023 is the near limit and z = -0.023 the far one. Returns
    (standoffs (m,), side) where side is "near", "far" or None for where the
    median sits relative to the guarded window -- RULE B's input.
    """
    z = np.asarray(pts_s, float).reshape(-1, 3)[:, 2]
    d = ref - z[np.isfinite(z)]
    if d.size == 0:
        return d, None
    med = float(np.median(d))
    lo, hi = window
    return d, ("near" if med < lo else ("far" if med > hi else None))


def _gauge_plane_fit(xy, delta):
    """(offset, tilt_mrad or None, rms) of a plane through (x, y) -> delta.

    UNITS, BECAUSE THEY DO NOT MATCH AND THE TILT IS WHERE THAT BITES. `xy` is
    in METRES and `delta` in MILLIMETRES, which is how both arrive and how both
    are reported -- so the fitted gradient is mm per metre and has to be
    divided by a thousand before an angle is taken from it. Left unconverted, a
    40 mrad tilt comes out as 1546 mrad (88.6 degrees) -- not a subtle wrong
    answer, but one that is easy to mistake for a frame convention problem.

    Fitted on xy RELATIVE TO THE CENTROID, so the constant term is the mean
    disagreement over the samples rather than an extrapolation to the origin --
    at a metre of reach an ill-conditioned intercept is the difference between
    a number and a rumour.

    Three points fit a plane EXACTLY, so the rms is 0.0 there by construction
    and is not evidence of anything. It starts carrying information at n >= 4.
    """
    A = np.asarray(xy, float).reshape(-1, 2)
    d = np.asarray(delta, float).reshape(-1)
    n = d.size
    if n == 0:
        return float("nan"), None, float("nan")
    c = A.mean(axis=0)
    if n < 3:
        off = float(d.mean())
        return off, None, float(math.sqrt(float(((d - off)**2).mean())))
    M = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), A[:, 0] - c[0], A[:, 1] - c[1]])
    coef, _res, rank, _sv = np.linalg.lstsq(M, d, rcond=None)
    if rank < 3:
        # Collinear samples. The gradient across the line is not measured, so
        # reporting a tilt from it would be reporting the fit's own arbitrary
        # choice -- the 90.06 degree answer in the header, one dimension down.
        off = float(d.mean())
        return off, None, float(math.sqrt(float(((d - off)**2).mean())))
    resid = d - M.dot(coef)
    rms = float(math.sqrt(float((resid**2).mean())))
    grad = 1e-3*math.hypot(float(coef[1]), float(coef[2]))   # mm/m -> m/m
    tilt = 1e3*math.atan(grad)
    return float(coef[0]), tilt, rms


def _gauge_save(res, take, say):
    """The samples and the verdict, inside the take. Never raises.

    ONE FILE, because the expensive half of this is the arm time and the cheap
    half is the arithmetic: a gauge run that produced a number nobody can
    re-derive next week has to be flown again.
    """
    if take is None or not getattr(take, "dir", None):
        return None
    try:
        d = os.path.join(take.dir, "azure_gauge")
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        path = os.path.join(d, "gauge.json")
        text = json.dumps(res, indent=2, sort_keys=True, default=float)
        _cal_atomic_write(path, lambda f: f.write(text.encode("utf-8")))
        say("      saved %s" % (path,))
        return path
    except Exception as e:                                    # noqa: BLE001
        say("      (the gauge result could not be saved: %s: %s)"
            % (type(e).__name__, e))
        return None


def gauge_mesh_against_keyence(mesh=None, points=None, n=3, start_standoff=None,
                               step=0.020, tries=3, say=None, take=None,
                               region=None, confirm_moves=False):
    """MEASURE HOW WRONG THE AZURE MESH IS, WITH THE KEYENCE, ON THE PART.

    AN OPTICAL GAUGE. NOTHING HERE TOUCHES THE PART, ever, and nothing here
    needs a contact touch-off or an operator's judgement. The Keyence is a
    triangulation profiler standing off 57..103 mm and every pose in this
    routine is placed to keep it there with margin. Do not add a contact step.

    AND IT DOES NOT PROMPT. `confirm_moves=False`, the default, is the route
    run_automated_scan() gets: the same primitives the Azure sweep and the
    Keyence raster fly, with the work volume, the cable band and the speed limit
    asked for explicitly before anything is commanded, and nothing to answer.
    The autonomous pipeline must never ask an operator to approve a move.
    `confirm_moves=True` is for a hand call at the dgpy prompt, which is
    calibration-ish: it flies _cal_fly_or_refuse() exactly as
    verify_calibration() does, so every move is previewed, confirmed and watched
    by _Monitor. See the header for which route keeps which guard.

    It flies the Keyence over `n` places on the mesh, one after another, and at
    each one subtracts the mesh's own predicted height from the height the
    sensor measures:

        delta = z_keyence - z_mesh      POSITIVE MEANS THE MESH READS LOW

    which is the direction of this cell's standing +12.45 mm bias. A caller
    gates on abs(dz_mm) <= AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM before planning a Keyence raster
    over an Azure mesh. Ungated, a raster over a mesh 13.6 mm low flies the head
    at 58 mm against a 57 mm limit and brings 26-39% of a surface-ground plate
    back as noise, which is then meshed.

    Arguments:

      mesh            anything _surface_geometry() accepts -- a PLY path, a
                      (verts, tris) pair, (verts, normals, tris), a world z or
                      {"plane_z": z}. Defaults to the session's `rough_mesh`.
                      REFUSES rather than guessing when there is none.
      points          where to measure, instead of letting the mesh choose:
                      (x, y) pairs (z comes from the mesh) or (x, y, z) triples
                      (taken as the mesh's prediction, so no mesh is needed).
      n               how many places. 3 or more gives a tilt as well as an
                      offset; ignored when `points` is given.
      start_standoff  head-to-predicted-surface for the first grab, metres.
                      Default AZURE_GAUGE_START_STANDOFF, 0.100 -- the far end
                      of the window, which is what leaves 43 mm of headroom on
                      the side that hurts.
      step            how far the head steps DOWN after a grab that returned
                      nothing, metres. Must be under the 46 mm window width.
      tries           how many rungs of that ladder, at most. Three cover
                      -43 .. +43 mm of mesh error; see the header.
      say             where the running commentary goes. Default _cal_say.
      take            a capture_scenario()'s take, if the caller has one. Given
                      one, the result is written into it AND THE LASER IS THE
                      CALLER'S; with none, this switches the Keyence laser on
                      and off around the measurement itself.
      region          (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) in world metres: where the PART
                      is, so the samples are spread over it rather than over the
                      mesh, which extends as far as the sweep integrated.
                      Default None takes `detected_region`, which stage 1 of
                      run_automated_scan() sets on both its branches and which
                      is therefore already correct at stage 2a. `region=False`
                      spreads them over the whole mesh, which on a TSDF mesh can
                      put every one of them on the bench.
                      Ignored when `points` names the places explicitly.
      confirm_moves   False (default, AND WHAT THE PIPELINE MUST USE): fly the
                      unattended route, no prompt. True: fly
                      _cal_fly_or_refuse(), which previews and asks the operator
                      before every move -- right for a hand call, wrong for an
                      autonomous run.

    Returns a dict:

      ok            bool   every sampled point produced a valid Keyence reading
      n_measured    int
      n_requested   int
      dz_mm         float  mean (keyence - mesh); + means the mesh reads LOW
      tilt_mrad     float  from a plane fit across the samples -- None when
                           there are fewer than three of them, when they turn
                           out collinear, or when their xy spread is under
                           AZURE_GAUGE_MIN_SPREAD_M and a tilt would be noise
                           rather than a measurement. CHECK FOR None.
      residual_mm   float  rms of the samples about that plane. Exactly 0.0 at
                           n == 3, which fits a plane exactly.
      samples       list   per point: xy, z_mesh, z_keyence, standoff_used,
                           tries_used, n_profiles, n_points, and the rest
      why           str    empty when ok, else why it stopped

    plus dz_sd_mm, tilt_resolution_mrad, spread_mm, within_tolerance,
    tolerance_mm, mesh_correction_mm, implied_standoff_mm,
    margin_to_limit_mm, rungs_mm, surface, region, region_from,
    confirm_moves and notes.

    About a minute for three points. Nothing is solved, written to a URDF or
    deployed: this MEASURES, and what to do about the number is the caller's.
    """
    say = _gauge_say(say)
    ref = float(CAL_KEYENCE_REF_DIST)
    lo_hard, hi_hard = (float(CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[0]),
                        float(CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_M[1]))
    margin = float(CAL_KEYENCE_RANGE_MARGIN_M)
    s0 = float(AZURE_GAUGE_START_STANDOFF if start_standoff is None
               else start_standoff)
    rungs, ladder_why = gauge_standoff_rungs(start=s0, step=step, tries=tries)
    # WHERE THE PART IS, resolved before anything is chosen or flown, so a
    # region that is malformed or describes another part is a refusal with the
    # arm still parked. See _gauge_region() and the header.
    #
    # NOT RESOLVED AT ALL when `points` names the places: the caller being
    # explicit leaves the region nothing to add, and recording one in the saved
    # result that had no effect on where the head went would be a lie in the
    # only file that says what this measurement was.
    if points is None:
        reg, reg_from = _gauge_region(region)
    else:
        reg, reg_from = None, ("the %d place(s) the caller named"
                               % (len(points),))
        if region is not None and region is not False:
            say("    (region= is ignored: points= says where to measure)")

    # -- where to measure ---------------------------------------------------
    geom, what = (None, None)
    aim = None
    if points is not None:
        P = np.asarray(points, float).reshape(len(points), -1)
        if P.shape[1] == 3:
            aim = P.astype(float)
            if mesh is not None:
                geom, what = _gauge_surface(mesh)
            else:
                what = "the %d point(s) given" % (aim.shape[0],)
        elif P.shape[1] == 2:
            geom, what = _gauge_surface(mesh)
            if geom[0] == "plane":
                aim = np.column_stack([P, np.full(P.shape[0], float(geom[1]))])
            else:
                # Nearest vertex in xy, which is the same rule
                # gauge_sample_points() uses and for the same reason: the
                # vertex's own z IS the mesh's prediction there.
                V = np.asarray(geom[1], float).reshape(-1, 3)
                idx = [int(np.argmin((V[:, 0] - x)**2 + (V[:, 1] - y)**2))
                       for x, y in P]
                aim = V[idx]
        else:
            raise CalibrationRefused(
                "points= must be (x, y) pairs or (x, y, z) triples; got %d "
                "columns." % (P.shape[1],))
    else:
        geom, what = _gauge_surface(mesh)
        env = None
        fn = globals().get("capture_envelope")
        if fn is not None:
            try:
                env = fn()
            except Exception:                                 # noqa: BLE001
                env = None
        aim = gauge_sample_points(geom, n=n, envelope=env, region=reg)
    n_req = int(aim.shape[0])
    # WHAT THE GATE IS HANDED. _surface_geometry() parses a PLY from disk every
    # time it is called, and the gate calls it once per pose -- so the surface
    # is handed on in the form it has already been parsed INTO, which that same
    # function accepts unchanged: a (verts, tris) pair, or the plane's z.
    surface = None if geom is None else (
        float(geom[1]) if geom[0] == "plane" else (geom[1], geom[2]))

    window = (lo_hard + margin, hi_hard - margin)
    prof_topic = sensor_topic("keyence", "profiles", "/profiles")
    q_home = _cal_tool_quat(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
    # THE SENSOR'S PLACE AND ITS AXIS, READ FROM LIVE TF AND NOT ASSUMED. The
    # optical frame's -z is the measuring direction (the head sits at
    # +keyence_ref_dist along its +z), so the third column of the probe_end ->
    # optical rotation, negated, is the look direction in the probe_end frame.
    # On the ec tooling that rotation is a pure Rz(+90 deg) and it comes out as
    # probe_end's -z exactly -- but a cell that tilts the head gets the tilt
    # for free, which is the whole reason it is read.
    T_ps = _cal_tf("probe_end", "lj_v7080_optical_frame")
    axis = -np.asarray(T_ps, float)[:3, 2]

    res = {"ok": False, "n_measured": 0, "n_requested": n_req,
           "dz_mm": float("nan"), "tilt_mrad": None,
           "residual_mm": float("nan"), "samples": [], "why": "",
           "dz_sd_mm": float("nan"), "tilt_resolution_mrad": None,
           "spread_mm": 0.0, "tolerance_mm": float(AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM),
           "within_tolerance": False, "mesh_correction_mm": float("nan"),
           "implied_standoff_mm": float("nan"),
           "margin_to_limit_mm": float("nan"),
           "start_standoff_mm": 1e3*s0,
           "rungs_mm": [1e3*v for v in rungs],
           "ladder": ladder_why, "surface": what,
           "ref_dist_mm": 1e3*ref,
           "window_mm": [1e3*window[0], 1e3*window[1]],
           "region": None if reg is None else list(reg),
           "region_from": reg_from,
           "confirm_moves": bool(confirm_moves),
           "notes": []}

    say("")
    say("  AZURE MESH vs THE KEYENCE, over %s" % (what,))
    if points is not None:
        say("    at the %d place(s) the caller named" % (n_req,))
    elif reg is None:
        say("    sampled over %s -- NOT over a region. A reconstructed mesh "
            "reaches as far as the sweep integrated, so on a part smaller than "
            "the mesh this CAN MEASURE THE BENCH INSTEAD OF THE PART."
            % (reg_from,))
    else:
        say("    sampled inside %s, x %.3f..%.3f y %.3f..%.3f"
            % (reg_from, reg[0], reg[2], reg[1], reg[3]))
    say("    moves: %s"
        % ("CONFIRMED -- the operator approves each one (confirm_moves=True)"
           if confirm_moves else
           "unattended, on the pipeline's own motion path; no prompt"))
    say("    %d point(s); the head starts %.0f mm off the mesh-predicted "
        "surface (the %.0f mm reference distance %+.0f mm), which returns for a "
        "mesh error of %+.0f .. %+.0f mm"
        % (n_req, 1e3*s0, 1e3*ref, 1e3*(s0 - ref),
           1e3*(s0 - hi_hard), 1e3*(s0 - lo_hard)))
    if len(rungs) > 1:
        say("    fallback ladder %s mm, for a mesh that reads HIGH and puts "
            "the surface beyond the far limit; %s"
            % (", ".join("%.0f" % (1e3*v) for v in rungs), ladder_why))
    say("    NOTHING HERE TOUCHES THE PART: it is an optical gauge.")

    own_laser = take is None and globals().get("capture_laser") is not None
    if own_laser:
        capture_laser(True)
    said_kinds = set()
    try:
        for i in range(n_req):
            a = np.asarray(aim[i], float).reshape(3)
            label = "gauge %d/%d at (%.3f, %.3f)" % (i + 1, n_req, a[0], a[1])
            # THE OPERATOR'S ONLY CONTROL DURING AN UNATTENDED RUN IS THE
            # PENDANT, and this is where stopping External Control becomes a
            # hold rather than four moves issued at a deactivated controller --
            # the same call _fly_scanpath() makes at every sweep row boundary.
            # No-op when robot_supervisor.dpi is not loaded, and nearly free.
            _ckpt = globals().get("scan_checkpoint")
            if callable(_ckpt):
                _ckpt("azure gauge point %d/%d" % (i + 1, n_req))
            s_row = {"xy": [float(a[0]), float(a[1])],
                     "z_mesh": float(a[2]), "z_keyence": None,
                     "standoff_used": None, "tries_used": 0,
                     "n_profiles": 0, "n_points": 0, "label": label,
                     "delta_mm": None, "standoff_measured_mm": None,
                     "delta_from_standoff_mm": None, "ok": False, "why": "",
                     "scatter_mm": None, "side": None}
            tried = []
            for k, s in enumerate(rungs):
                s_row["tries_used"] = k + 1
                # RULE A, as the gate's own near limit. The far end is left at
                # the sensor's own: a gauge stands off at the far end of the
                # window on purpose and must not be graded as a raster.
                delta_max = 0.0 if k == 0 else (rungs[k - 1] - hi_hard)
                near_floor = lo_hard + margin + delta_max
                xyz = _cal_pose_for_sensor(a, q_home, s - ref,
                                           "lj_v7080_optical_frame")
                if surface is not None:
                    # THROUGH THE EXISTING GATE, not around it: it casts the
                    # sensor's axis at the mesh and measures how far the FIRST
                    # surface really is, which is not the same question as how
                    # far this pose intended it to be. A nearer piece of the
                    # part between the head and the vertex that was aimed at,
                    # or a ray that finds nothing because the aim is past the
                    # mesh's edge, show up here and nowhere else.
                    #
                    # GRADED AGAINST THE SENSOR'S OWN 57..103 mm, margin zero,
                    # and RULE A is applied separately below. A gauge stands
                    # off at the FAR end of the window on purpose, so the
                    # symmetric guard a raster is held to would refuse its
                    # first rung for being 100 mm; and narrowing the gate's
                    # window by handing it a different range_m would make its
                    # own printed line ("the LJ-V7080 measures from ... ") say
                    # something untrue. Two checks, each saying what it means.
                    #
                    # A REFUSAL HERE COSTS THIS SAMPLE POINT AND NOT THE RUN,
                    # and it does NOT step down: the commanded standoff falls
                    # by 20 mm at the next rung, so a pose refused for a nearer
                    # surface is refused harder one rung down. Nothing moved.
                    try:
                        keyence_standoff_gate(
                            _GaugePath([xyz], [q_home]), surface,
                            margin=0.0, range_m=(lo_hard, hi_hard),
                            what="%s, head at %.0f mm" % (label, 1e3*s),
                            quiet=True, drop=0.0,
                            tf_offset=np.asarray(T_ps, float)[:3, 3],
                            axis=axis, ref_dist=ref)
                    except ValueError as e:
                        why1 = str(e).splitlines()[0]
                        tried.append("%.0f mm: refused by the standoff gate "
                                     "before flying -- %s" % (1e3*s, why1))
                        say("    %s at %.0f mm: REFUSED before flying -- %s"
                            % (label, 1e3*s, why1))
                        break
                    # RULE A, against what the RAY found rather than against
                    # what the pose asked for. The gate has just stored its
                    # report; a surface the mesh puts nearer than the sampled
                    # vertex is exactly the case where those two differ, and it
                    # is the case that ends with the head close to the part.
                    d_ray = last_standoff_report["standoff"]
                    d_ray = float(np.asarray(d_ray).reshape(-1)[0])
                    if np.isfinite(d_ray) and d_ray < near_floor:
                        why1 = ("the mesh puts the first surface %.1f mm from "
                                "the head, under the %.1f mm floor this rung "
                                "may command (the %.0f mm near limit, its "
                                "%.0f mm margin, and %.0f mm of mesh error "
                                "already ruled out)"
                                % (1e3*d_ray, 1e3*near_floor, 1e3*lo_hard,
                                   1e3*margin, -1e3*delta_max))
                        tried.append("%.0f mm: refused by the standoff gate "
                                     "before flying -- %s" % (1e3*s, why1))
                        say("    %s at %.0f mm: REFUSED before flying -- %s"
                            % (label, 1e3*s, why1))
                        break
                # THE SAME TWO ROUTES verify_calibration's Keyence legs fly --
                # the straight line, then the same endpoint in joint space, then
                # a refusal that names every route tried -- carrying the work
                # volume, the cable band and the speed limit either way. WHICH
                # of the two is chosen by confirm_moves, and the default is the
                # one that does not stop to ask: see _gauge_move() and the
                # header. The pipeline is never prompted.
                #
                # A POSE THAT CANNOT BE REACHED COSTS THIS SAMPLE POINT, not the
                # run: the other points are still a measurement, and a gauge
                # that refuses the whole part because one corner is out of reach
                # is a gauge nobody runs. It does not step down either --
                # reachability is not what the ladder is for.
                try:
                    _gauge_move(xyz, q_home,
                                "%s, head at %.0f mm" % (label, 1e3*s),
                                confirm_moves=confirm_moves)
                except CalibrationRefused as e:
                    why1 = str(e).splitlines()[0]
                    tried.append("%.0f mm: the arm did not reach it -- %s"
                                 % (1e3*s, why1))
                    say("    %s at %.0f mm: NOT REACHED -- %s"
                        % (label, 1e3*s, why1))
                    break
                time.sleep(CAL_SETTLE_S)
                if "_CAL" in globals():
                    _CAL["last_profile_check"] = None
                try:
                    pts_s, frame, k_msg = _cal_profile_points(prof_topic)
                except CalibrationRefused as e:
                    chk = (globals().get("_CAL") or {}).get("last_profile_check")
                    why1 = str(e).splitlines()[0]
                    tried.append("%.0f mm: %s" % (1e3*s, why1))
                    side = None
                    if chk and chk.get("standoff") is not None:
                        d_med = float(chk["standoff"])
                        side = ("near" if d_med < lo_hard + margin
                                else ("far" if d_med > hi_hard - margin
                                      else None))
                        s_row["side"] = side
                    say("    %s at %.0f mm: no usable profile -- %s"
                        % (label, 1e3*s, why1))
                    # RULE B. The sensor's own heights say the surface is
                    # INSIDE the near limit, so this is not the far-side case
                    # the ladder is for and stepping down would close on the
                    # part. Stop the whole run and say so.
                    #
                    # IT CANNOT ALWAYS FIRE, and that is the honest limit of
                    # this check: out-of-range columns come back as +Inf, so a
                    # surface wholly inside the near limit returns NO finite
                    # points and check_profile has no standoff to report. RULE
                    # B catches the partial case -- part of the line in range,
                    # the median below the guard -- which is the common one on
                    # anything tilted or stepped. The total case is what RULE C
                    # bounds instead, by refusing to descend past the point
                    # where that reading would imply contact.
                    if side == "near":
                        res["why"] = (
                            "%s: the sensor's own heights put the surface "
                            "%.1f mm from the head, inside the %.0f mm near "
                            "limit. THE MESH READS LOW BY AT LEAST %.1f mm. "
                            "The ladder steps the head DOWN and that would "
                            "close on the part, so nothing further has been "
                            "flown. Fix the Azure correction before planning "
                            "any Keyence raster over this mesh."
                            % (label, 1e3*float(chk["standoff"]), 1e3*lo_hard,
                               1e3*(s - float(chk["standoff"]))))
                        s_row["why"] = res["why"]
                        res["samples"].append(s_row)
                        raise CalibrationRefused(res["why"])
                    continue
                d_meas, side = _gauge_profile_facts(pts_s, ref, window)
                s_row["side"] = side
                T = _cal_tf("world", frame)
                world = pts_s.dot(T[:3, :3].T) + T[:3, 3]
                z_key = float(np.median(world[:, 2]))
                s_row.update({
                    "z_keyence": z_key, "standoff_used": float(s),
                    "n_profiles": int(k_msg), "n_points": int(world.shape[0]),
                    "delta_mm": 1e3*(z_key - float(a[2])),
                    "standoff_measured_mm": (1e3*float(np.median(d_meas))
                                             if d_meas.size else None),
                    "scatter_mm": 1e3*float(np.std(world[:, 2])),
                    "ok": True})
                if d_meas.size:
                    s_row["delta_from_standoff_mm"] = 1e3*(
                        s - float(np.median(d_meas)))
                    rep = keyence_range_report(d_meas, commanded=s,
                                               n_profiles=int(k_msg))
                    # SAID ONCE, NOT ONCE PER POINT. A uniform mesh error is
                    # one fact, and describe_range_report() carries `skip` for
                    # exactly this -- the paragraph is what is suppressed, not
                    # the measurement, and the gauge's own line below still
                    # gives the number at every point.
                    for line in describe_range_report(
                            rep, what="%s, head at %.0f mm" % (label, 1e3*s),
                            skip=tuple(said_kinds)):
                        say(line)
                    said_kinds.update(rep.get("why_kinds") or ())
                    gap = abs((s_row["delta_from_standoff_mm"] or 0.0)
                              - s_row["delta_mm"])
                    if gap > 1e3*AZURE_GAUGE_TF_TOL_M:
                        note = ("%s: the world-frame and sensor-frame readings "
                                "of the disagreement differ by %.2f mm "
                                "(%.2f vs %.2f). TF and the commanded pose do "
                                "not agree -- a stale transform, a description "
                                "that moved, or an arm that did not arrive. The "
                                "world-frame number is the one used."
                                % (label, gap, s_row["delta_mm"],
                                   s_row["delta_from_standoff_mm"]))
                        res["notes"].append(note)
                        say("    ** %s" % (note,))
                sm = s_row["standoff_measured_mm"]
                say("    %s: mesh z %.4f, keyence z %.4f, so the mesh reads "
                    "%+.2f mm %s (head commanded %.0f mm, measuring at "
                    "%s mm; %d points from %d profiles)"
                    % (label, a[2], z_key, s_row["delta_mm"],
                       "LOW" if s_row["delta_mm"] > 0 else "high", 1e3*s,
                       "--" if sm is None else "%.1f" % (sm,),
                       s_row["n_points"], s_row["n_profiles"]))
                # THE GRAB WORKED AND THE HEAD IS IN THE GUARD BAND. Not a
                # ladder decision -- the answer is in hand and this point is
                # done -- but it means the mesh reads low by nearly the whole
                # of the headroom this gauge was given, and the next thing the
                # operator must not do is plan a raster at 80 mm.
                if side == "near":
                    note = ("%s measured at %.1f mm, inside the %.0f mm near "
                            "limit plus its %.0f mm margin. The mesh reads low "
                            "by %.1f mm, nearly the whole %.0f mm of headroom "
                            "this gauge starts with. A raster planned over it "
                            "at the %.0f mm reference distance would fly "
                            "INSIDE the hard limit."
                            % (label, sm if sm is not None else float("nan"),
                               1e3*lo_hard, 1e3*margin, s_row["delta_mm"],
                               1e3*(s - lo_hard), 1e3*ref))
                    res["notes"].append(note)
                    say("    ** %s" % (note,))
                break
            if not s_row["ok"]:
                s_row["why"] = ("no rung of the ladder returned a usable "
                                "profile: " + "; ".join(tried))
                if not res["why"]:
                    res["why"] = "%s: %s" % (label, s_row["why"])
            res["samples"].append(s_row)
    except _gauge_stops() as e:
        # THESE END THE RUN AND ARE NOT THIS FUNCTION'S TO SWALLOW. A monitor
        # stop means a human has to look at the work cell before the arm moves
        # again; the taxonomy is in cell_capture_node.dpi. ScanAborted is in the
        # tuple because the unattended route's only operator control is the
        # pendant -- see _gauge_stops().
        res["why"] = _cal_stop_why(e)
        _gauge_finalise(res, say, ref, lo_hard)
        _gauge_save(res, take, say)
        globals()["last_gauge_result"] = res
        raise
    except CalibrationRefused:
        _gauge_finalise(res, say, ref, lo_hard)
        _gauge_save(res, take, say)
        globals()["last_gauge_result"] = res
        raise
    finally:
        if own_laser:
            capture_laser(False)

    _gauge_finalise(res, say, ref, lo_hard)
    _gauge_save(res, take, say)
    if take is not None and hasattr(take, "results"):
        try:
            take.results.update({"azure_gauge": res})
        except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
            pass
    globals()["last_gauge_result"] = res
    return res


global _gauge_finalise
def _gauge_finalise(res, say, ref, lo_hard):
    """Reduce the samples to the verdict, and print it. Never raises.

    SEPARATE FROM THE FLYING because the interesting paths out are the ones
    where it stopped early: a run that measured two of three points still
    knows two thirds of the answer, and an operator standing at a cell reading
    a refusal needs that printed rather than thrown away.
    """
    good = [s for s in res["samples"] if s.get("ok")]
    res["n_measured"] = len(good)
    res["ok"] = bool(good) and len(good) == res["n_requested"] and not res["why"]
    if not good:
        if not res["why"]:
            res["why"] = ("no sampled point produced a valid Keyence reading, "
                          "so nothing has been measured.")
        say("    NOTHING MEASURED: %s" % (res["why"],))
        return res
    xy = np.asarray([s["xy"] for s in good], float)
    d = np.asarray([s["delta_mm"] for s in good], float)
    off, tilt, rms = _gauge_plane_fit(xy, d)
    dx = float(xy[:, 0].max() - xy[:, 0].min()) if len(xy) else 0.0
    dy = float(xy[:, 1].max() - xy[:, 1].min()) if len(xy) else 0.0
    spread = math.hypot(dx, dy)
    res["spread_mm"] = 1e3*spread
    res["dz_mm"] = float(d.mean())
    res["dz_sd_mm"] = float(np.std(d))
    res["residual_mm"] = float(rms)
    scat = [s["scatter_mm"] for s in good if s.get("scatter_mm") is not None]
    if scat and spread > 0.0:
        res["tilt_resolution_mrad"] = float(
            (sum(scat)/len(scat))/res["spread_mm"]*1e3)
    if tilt is not None and spread < float(AZURE_GAUGE_MIN_SPREAD_M):
        note = ("the samples span %.0f mm, under the %.0f mm this file will "
                "report a tilt over. A tilt is a height difference divided by "
                "a baseline, and over a short baseline it is the sensor's "
                "noise -- verify_calibration reported 15.0 mrad on a patch "
                "whose true tilt was 1.1 mrad. The offset below is unaffected."
                % (res["spread_mm"], 1e3*AZURE_GAUGE_MIN_SPREAD_M))
        res["notes"].append(note)
        say("    ** %s" % (note,))
        tilt = None
    res["tilt_mrad"] = tilt
    res["mesh_correction_mm"] = res["dz_mm"]
    res["within_tolerance"] = bool(abs(res["dz_mm"]) <= res["tolerance_mm"])
    # WHAT IT COSTS, in the units the failure arrives in: a raster planned over
    # this mesh puts the optical frame on the mesh, so the head flies at the
    # reference distance MINUS the disagreement.
    flown = ref - 1e-3*res["dz_mm"]
    res["implied_standoff_mm"] = 1e3*flown
    res["margin_to_limit_mm"] = 1e3*(flown - lo_hard)
    say("")
    say("    %d of %d point(s) measured over %.0f mm"
        % (res["n_measured"], res["n_requested"], res["spread_mm"]))
    say("    THE MESH READS %+.2f mm %s (sd %.2f over the points)"
        % (res["dz_mm"], "LOW" if res["dz_mm"] > 0 else "HIGH",
           res["dz_sd_mm"]))
    if res["tilt_mrad"] is None:
        say("    tilt: not measured (fewer than three usable points, "
            "collinear, or too short a baseline)")
    else:
        say("    tilt %.2f mrad across the samples, %.3f mm rms about that "
            "plane%s"
            % (res["tilt_mrad"], res["residual_mm"],
               "" if res["tilt_resolution_mrad"] is None else
               " (the sensor's own scatter over this baseline is %.2f mrad, "
               "so that is the floor)" % (res["tilt_resolution_mrad"],)))
    say("    CONSEQUENCE: a Keyence raster planned over this mesh would fly at "
        "%.1f mm, %.1f mm clear of the head's %.0f mm hard limit."
        % (res["implied_standoff_mm"], res["margin_to_limit_mm"], 1e3*lo_hard))
    # THE TWO WAYS THIS FAILS ARE NOT THE SAME FAILURE and must not print the
    # same sentence. `within_tolerance` is about the MESH; `ok` is about whether
    # the gauge got to finish. Down one branch, a run that measured 2 of 3
    # points and found the mesh 0.70 mm out -- well inside a 2.0 mm tolerance --
    # would print "OVER 2.0 mm ... Correct the Azure depth by -0.70 mm", which
    # is a correction nobody needs for a fault that was a wrist angle. An
    # operator who acts on that line reconstructs again and gets the same
    # refusal.
    if res["within_tolerance"] and res["ok"]:
        say("    WITHIN %.1f mm: the mesh may be rastered over."
            % (res["tolerance_mm"],))
    elif not res["within_tolerance"]:
        say("    OVER %.1f mm%s. DO NOT PLAN A KEYENCE RASTER OVER THIS MESH: "
            "a raster over a mesh this wrong flies the head inside its near "
            "limit and loses 26-39%% of the surface. "
            "Correct the Azure depth by %+.2f mm and re-run the "
            "reconstruction, or raster over a better surface."
            % (res["tolerance_mm"],
               "" if res["ok"] else " (and only %d of %d points measured, so "
               "this number is from fewer points than were asked for)"
               % (res["n_measured"], res["n_requested"]),
               res["mesh_correction_mm"]))
    else:
        say("    INCOMPLETE: %d of %d points measured. What WAS measured is "
            "%+.2f mm, inside the %.1f mm tolerance -- so the mesh is not the "
            "thing that failed here and correcting the Azure depth would not "
            "help. The reason the rest was not measured is below; fix that and "
            "gauge again."
            % (res["n_measured"], res["n_requested"], res["dz_mm"],
               res["tolerance_mm"]))
    if res["why"]:
        say("    STOPPED: %s" % (res["why"],))
    return res


def describe_gauge(res=None):
    """The last (or a given) gauge result as lines, for a report or a log."""
    res = last_gauge_result if res is None else res
    if not res:
        return ["  no gauge has been run in this session"]
    lines = ["  azure mesh vs the keyence over %s" % (res.get("surface"),),
             "    %d of %d point(s) measured over %.0f mm; the mesh reads "
             "%+.2f mm %s (sd %.2f)"
             % (res.get("n_measured", 0), res.get("n_requested", 0),
                res.get("spread_mm", 0.0), res.get("dz_mm", float("nan")),
                "LOW" if (res.get("dz_mm") or 0.0) > 0 else "HIGH",
                res.get("dz_sd_mm", float("nan")))]
    if res.get("region"):
        r = res["region"]
        lines.append("    sampled inside %s, x %.3f..%.3f y %.3f..%.3f"
                     % (res.get("region_from") or "a region",
                        r[0], r[2], r[1], r[3]))
    elif res.get("region_from"):
        lines.append("    sampled over %s" % (res["region_from"],))
    if res.get("tilt_mrad") is not None:
        lines.append("    tilt %.2f mrad, %.3f mm rms about the plane"
                     % (res["tilt_mrad"], res.get("residual_mm",
                                                  float("nan"))))
    for s in res.get("samples", ()):
        lines.append("      (%.3f, %.3f) mesh %.4f keyence %s  %s mm  "
                     "head %s mm, %d tr%s"
                     % (s["xy"][0], s["xy"][1], s["z_mesh"],
                        "--" if s.get("z_keyence") is None
                        else "%.4f" % s["z_keyence"],
                        "--" if s.get("delta_mm") is None
                        else "%+.2f" % s["delta_mm"],
                        "--" if s.get("standoff_used") is None
                        else "%.0f" % (1e3*s["standoff_used"]),
                        s.get("tries_used", 0),
                        "" if s.get("ok") else "  FAILED: %s" % (s.get("why"),)))
    for note in res.get("notes", ()):
        lines.append("    ** %s" % (note,))
    if res.get("why"):
        lines.append("    STOPPED: %s" % (res["why"],))
    return lines
